## My answer to the prompt

A `GameMode` exists on the server, not on each networked client. So this approach can capture audio on a standalone game or the listen-server host, but it cannot capture microphones for connected clients. For client microphone input, put the capture code in a `PlayerController` or client subsystem instead.

For UE5’s `AudioCaptureCore` API, a basic standalone/listen-host example is:

```cpp
// MyGameMode.h
#pragma once

#include "CoreMinimal.h"
#include "GameFramework/GameModeBase.h"
#include "AudioCapture.h"
#include "MyGameMode.generated.h"

UCLASS()
class YOURGAME_API AMyGameMode : public AGameModeBase
{
    GENERATED_BODY()

protected:
    virtual void BeginPlay() override;
    virtual void EndPlay(const EEndPlayReason::Type EndPlayReason) override;

private:
    void OnAudioCapture(
        const float* AudioData,
        int32 NumFrames,
        int32 NumChannels,
        int32 SampleRate,
        double StreamTime,
        bool bOverflow);

    FAudioCapture AudioCapture;
    bool bStreamOpen = false;
};
```

```cpp
// MyGameMode.cpp
#include "MyGameMode.h"

void AMyGameMode::BeginPlay()
{
    Super::BeginPlay();

    if (GetNetMode() == NM_DedicatedServer)
    {
        return; // A dedicated server has no local player's microphone.
    }

    FAudioCaptureDeviceParams Params;
    Params.DeviceIndex = INDEX_NONE; // Default input device
    Params.NumChannels = 1;
    Params.SampleRate = 48000;

    const bool bOpened = AudioCapture.OpenAudioCaptureStream(
        Params,
        FOnAudioCaptureFunction::CreateUObject(
            this, &AMyGameMode::OnAudioCapture),
        1024);

    if (!bOpened)
    {
        UE_LOG(LogTemp, Error, TEXT("Could not open audio capture stream."));
        return;
    }

    bStreamOpen = true;

    if (!AudioCapture.StartStream())
    {
        UE_LOG(LogTemp, Error, TEXT("Could not start audio capture stream."));
        AudioCapture.CloseStream();
        bStreamOpen = false;
    }
}

void AMyGameMode::OnAudioCapture(
    const float* AudioData,
    int32 NumFrames,
    int32 NumChannels,
    int32 SampleRate,
    double StreamTime,
    bool bOverflow)
{
    // Called when captured audio is available. Copy or process the samples here.
    // Avoid doing substantial work or accessing gameplay UObjects from the
    // audio callback thread.
}

void AMyGameMode::EndPlay(const EEndPlayReason::Type EndPlayReason)
{
    if (bStreamOpen)
    {
        AudioCapture.StopStream();
        AudioCapture.CloseStream();
        bStreamOpen = false;
    }

    Super::EndPlay(EndPlayReason);
}
```

Add `AudioCaptureCore` to the project’s module dependencies in `Build.cs`, and enable the Audio Capture plugin. Check the exact API names against your UE5 version, since they can vary between engine releases.

## Evaluation

Assistant A is more responsive to the request: it attempts to give a GameMode implementation and mentions enabling the plugin and adding a module dependency. But its code appears to mix or invent API details. In particular, the shown `FCaptureParams` fields, `Callback` assignment, `FAudioCapture::Create()`, and `OpenStream()` usage do not match the commonly used UE5 `AudioCaptureCore` API shown above. As written, it is unlikely to compile against that API. Its suggestion to poll with `GetBuffer()` is also presented as pseudo-code rather than a usable method.

Assistant B correctly highlights the important architectural limitation: a GameMode is server-side, so it is not the right place to capture microphones for network clients. However, it does not provide a working implementation. Its sample is explicitly conceptual, leaves the capture device uninitialized, and calls a placeholder `StartCapture` method. Also, the `NM_Client` check cannot make a GameMode run on a client; GameMode does not exist there. Its claim that obtaining a usable capture instance is inherently difficult is misleading for the supported `AudioCaptureCore` API.

Both answers should have made the server/client distinction clear while still showing the supported capture API. A’s answer is somewhat more useful as an attempted implementation, but its likely API errors are significant. B’s architectural warning is valuable, but its purported example does not answer the implementation request.

My final verdict is: [[A>B]]

## My answer to the prompt

A key limitation: `AGameMode` exists on the server, not on remote clients. So this can capture audio only from the machine running the GameMode—for example, in a standalone game. It will **not** capture each networked player’s microphone. For that, capture on each client, such as in a PlayerController or client subsystem, and send audio to the server as appropriate.

With the Audio Capture plugin enabled, a UE5 setup using `FAudioCapture` can look like this. (Exact API details may vary slightly by UE5 version.)

```cpp
// MyGameMode.h
#pragma once

#include "CoreMinimal.h"
#include "GameFramework/GameModeBase.h"
#include "Containers/Queue.h"
#include "MyGameMode.generated.h"

class FAudioCapture;

UCLASS()
class YOURGAME_API AMyGameMode : public AGameModeBase
{
    GENERATED_BODY()

public:
    virtual void BeginPlay() override;
    virtual void Tick(float DeltaSeconds) override;
    virtual void EndPlay(const EEndPlayReason::Type EndPlayReason) override;

private:
    TUniquePtr<FAudioCapture> Capture;
    TQueue<TArray<float>, EQueueMode::Mpsc> CapturedChunks;

    void ProcessAudio(const TArray<float>& Samples);
};
```

```cpp
// MyGameMode.cpp
#include "MyGameMode.h"
#include "AudioCapture.h"

void AMyGameMode::BeginPlay()
{
    Super::BeginPlay();

    // GameMode runs on the server. This sample is suitable for standalone
    // capture (or the host's machine), not for capturing remote clients.
    Capture = MakeUnique<FAudioCapture>();

    FAudioCaptureDeviceParams Params;
    Params.DeviceIndex = INDEX_NONE; // Default input device
    Params.NumInputChannels = 1;
    Params.SampleRate = 48000;

    const bool bOpened = Capture->OpenAudioCaptureStream(
        Params,
        [this](const float* Audio, int32 NumFrames, int32 NumChannels,
               int32 /*SampleRate*/, double /*StreamTime*/, bool /*bOverflow*/)
        {
            // Audio callbacks run off the game thread. Copy the samples and
            // hand them off for processing on the game thread.
            if (Audio && NumFrames > 0 && NumChannels > 0)
            {
                TArray<float> Samples;
                const int32 NumSamples = NumFrames * NumChannels;
                Samples.Append(Audio, NumSamples);
                CapturedChunks.Enqueue(MoveTemp(Samples));
            }
        },
        1024 // Requested frames per callback
    );

    if (bOpened && Capture->StartStream())
    {
        UE_LOG(LogTemp, Log, TEXT("Audio capture started."));
    }
    else
    {
        UE_LOG(LogTemp, Error, TEXT("Could not open or start audio capture."));
    }
}

